<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="557c60f7">&lt;p&gt;
    Listed below are a few key points to consider while creating and maintaining the Benefits Register work product:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The type of benefits that may be identified for an initiative will differ based on the subject matter.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Depending on the size and complexity of the initiative, a separate Benefits Register may be needed for each
        initiative/option being considered.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The details within the work product will likely be refined/elaborated as feedback is received from key
        stakeholders. However, care should be taken to verify that the scope of the initiative does not change due to the
        updates made to the Benefits Register.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px&quot; dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;
    As benefits measures are initially identified, the different attributes within the Benefits Register should be
    populated (as possible). Subsequently, as the measurement approach becomes more defined, the attributes in the register
    should be completed/refined.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style=&quot;MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px&quot; dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;
    Measures should be able to demonstrate that the relevant change has been delivered and the benefits have been realized.
    It is particularly useful for more complex changes where more than one measure may be used to track a program/project
    benefit.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;
    Benefit definitions should incorporate benefit measurement approaches, which should follow the following principles:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul style=&quot;MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px&quot; dir=&quot;ltr&quot;&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Ease of measurement – related data should be easily retrieved, and benefit calculations must follow simple set of
        principles
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Fairness and achievability – business units should be able to impact benefit measures by actions that are desirable
        for business operations
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Manifestation of program goals – benefit measures derive from program goals
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Consideration of business events – determine how ongoing business operations and decisions may have an impact on
        benefit.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="2d6650af"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="a7ff318b"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="9bea18e2">In addition to a clear description, an owner should also be listed for each benefit in the work
product. Additionally, each positive outcome should be categorized, ranked, and assigned a priority level.
Measures should be defined for each benefit and dis-benefit.</attribute>
